# =============================================================================
# analysis.R
# Workshop 6: remote studies with iMotions. Webcam eye tracking and webcam facial
# expression analysis (Affectiva AFFDEX) collected online through Remote Data Collection
# (RDC), built in iMotions Lab 11.1 and processed in Lab after download; analysis session
# Martin's Family Clothing Consumer Behavior Research Laboratory, JSU
#
# SIMULATED DATA. Every number this script reads was generated by make_simulated_data.py
# (the seed is printed in the first line of every file). No person was recorded. The design copies
# the logic of the iMotions vendor demo "(DEMO) Food Waste_webET_FEA_voice": every respondent saw
# two one minute food waste videos, a narrative one (foodwaste_narrative, 60 s) and an emotional
# visual one (foodwaste_visual, 62 s), in random order, then an infographic with four tips (15 s),
# then a questionnaire. Nothing from the demo's respondents was used.
#
# Design. 260 sessions opened a study link. Each order of the two videos is its own study flow:
# "Study flow 1" = narrative first, "Study flow 2" = visual first (the Block column of the session
# data). The flow was assigned at random before the link was opened (MODELED: the invitation list was
# split at random in two halves). Respondents passed the setup steps of the respondent funnel, a
# 13 point Pre-study calibration, both videos, the infographic, the questionnaire, and a 13 point
# Post-study calibration. Faces were scored in iMotions Lab (Post Processing > Affectiva, then FEA
# thresholding at 50); gaze on the infographic was analyzed with five AOIs (Headline, Tip_1 to Tip_4).
#
# Webcam eye tracking quality has two parts (iMotions Online data quality box):
#   accuracy = mean distance between where the respondent looked during calibration and where the
#              calibration point was, in degrees of visual angle (smaller is better);
#   quality  = valid eye tracking samples / all samples (percent); face quality = samples with a
#              face found / all samples (percent).
#
# Exclusion rule, written before collection (thresholds from the manuals):
#   exclude a completed respondent when the Pre-study OR the Post-study calibration accuracy is above
#   4.0 degrees (iMotions Online Study Setup: "Generally acceptable range: 0-4 dva"), when eye tracking
#   quality on the infographic is below 90 percent ("at least 90%"), or when face quality is below
#   80 percent during either video ("at least 80%").
#   Sensitivity: accuracy at or below 3.0 degrees at both calibrations ("We recommend a threshold of
#   3.0", Data collection and Analysis articles).
#
# Hypotheses (all tests two tailed, alpha = .05):
#   H1 (within): percent of time Engaged (AFFDEX Engagement above 50) is higher for the visual video
#      than for the narrative video. Paired t on the difference, Cohen's d_z with a 95% CI, Wilcoxon
#      signed rank check (percent of time data are skewed and often zero).
#   H2 (within, infographic): dwell time differs among the four tip AOIs. Friedman test with Kendall's
#      W, then Holm adjusted Wilcoxon signed rank comparisons of every pair; a repeated measures
#      descriptive table.
#   H3 (between; randomization check and order effect): intention to reduce food waste does not differ
#      by which video came first. Welch t with Hedges' g and its 95% CI.
#   Data quality analyses: the funnel table; completion by order (differential attrition, chi square);
#      Pre-study versus Post-study calibration accuracy (paired; drift); calibration accuracy and the
#      AOI hit on the smallest AOI (Headline).
#   Sensitivity: H1 and H2 with the stricter 3.0 degree threshold.
#   Exploratory: engagement difference (visual minus narrative) with the stated preference for the
#      visual video; tips recalled with dwell time on the tips.
#
# What the script does, in order:
#   STEP 0.  Load packages, set paths, the figure theme, and helper functions
#   STEP 1.  Read the five files
#   STEP 2.  Session data: status, device, browser, the "Eye tracking" text column
#   STEP 3.  The respondent funnel: counts and percents at every step
#   STEP 4.  The exclusion rule (quality table): exclusions by reason
#   STEP 5.  Differential attrition and exclusion by order (chi square and Fisher tests)
#   STEP 6.  Check the files against each other
#   STEP 7.  Calibration drift: Pre-study versus Post-study accuracy
#   STEP 8.  Analysis table: one row per kept respondent
#   STEP 9.  Descriptive statistics (SIMULATED_descriptives.csv)
#   STEP 10. H1: Engagement, visual versus narrative video
#   STEP 11. H2: dwell time on the four tips (Friedman, Kendall's W, Holm adjusted Wilcoxon)
#   STEP 12. H3: intention by order (randomization check)
#   STEP 13. Calibration accuracy and the hit on the smallest AOI (Headline)
#   STEP 14. Sensitivity: the 3.0 degree threshold
#   STEP 15. Exploratory: preference and engagement; recall and dwell
#   STEP 16. Assumption checks (figure)
#   STEP 17. Report figures (PNG, 1400 px wide, 150 dpi)
#   STEP 18. Summary table (SIMULATED_test_results.csv)
#
# MODELED layouts (verify on the lab computer; see the header line of each file): the CSV layout of
# "Download session data" and its three columns marked (modeled); the quality table
# (SIMULATED_quality.csv); the FEA Metrics Export header (the Workshop 3 layout); the identifier
# columns of the AOI export (the Workshop 2 layout); the survey export. Column names that ARE in the
# manuals: Id, Started, Ended, Eye tracking, Est. face %, Block, Status (RDC respondent list); the
# funnel step names; the AOI metric names.
#
# How to run (either way works):
#   a) In RStudio: open this file, set the working directory to the folder that holds the CSV
#      files (Session > Set Working Directory > To Source File Location), then Source.
#   b) From a terminal, inside the data folder:  Rscript analysis.R
# The console output is also written to analysis_output.txt next to the data.
#
# Packages used: readr, dplyr, tidyr, ggplot2, and grid (part of R). Effect sizes and their
# confidence intervals are computed by hand below; if the effectsize package happens to be
# installed, step 10 uses it only as a cross-check. If a package is missing, run once:
#   install.packages(c("readr", "dplyr", "tidyr", "ggplot2"))
# =============================================================================

